#
# The §B rows are the ones worth defending, because they are the format's
# whole point and the section had been empty: a bug with no row is a bug
# the next session repeats. Every other addition unblocks a task that was
# stalled on a decision nobody had made.
#
# Slack is ~12.5%, the same discipline the first ceiling used. When this
# one is next approached, compact §B before widening again -- oldest rows
# first, per the ONE FILE RULE.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS AT ALL, stated here because it is the trap it was
# born from: V12 required the ceiling from commit one, and for several
# commits this repo declared one in section C while nothing read it. A
# declaration no runner reads is a wish, and wishes drift silently because
# nothing reports them (V22). The runner landed with the number, in one
# commit, which is what V22 actually asks for.

